---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Prose from "../../components/Prose.astro";
import GuideData from "../../components/GuideData.astro";
import EveryCut from "../../components/EveryCut.astro";
import { bySlug, kicker } from "../../dossiers/registry.js";
import { dateStamp } from "../../dossiers/display.js";
const D = bySlug["iip"];
import { period, word } from "../../dossiers/periods.js";
import b22 from "../../../data/series/iip-2022.json";
import ind22 from "../../../data/series/iip-industries-2022.json";
import fy80 from "../../../data/series/iip-annual-1980.json";
import fy93 from "../../../data/series/iip-annual-1993.json";
import fy04 from "../../../data/series/iip-annual-2004.json";
import fy11 from "../../../data/series/iip-annual-2011.json";
import fy22 from "../../../data/series/iip-annual-2022.json";
import core11 from "../../../data/series/core-2011.json";
import core93 from "../../../data/series/core-1993.json";
// periods and counts named in the copy, read from the bundles (src/dossiers/periods.js)
const P = period("iip-2022"), PI = period("iip-industries-2022"), PISP = period("isp");
const cap = (t) => t[0].toUpperCase() + t.slice(1);
const gen = b22.series.general.data.filter((v) => v != null);
const nNeg = gen.filter((v) => v <= 0).length;
const rankWord = ["strongest", "second-strongest", "third-strongest"][[...gen].sort((a, b) => b - a).indexOf(gen.at(-1))] ?? null;
const last = (k) => b22.series[k].data.at(-1);
const pct = (v) => (v < 0 ? "−" + Math.abs(v).toFixed(2) : v.toFixed(2)) + "%";
// months capital goods has outgrown consumer non-durables, counted back from the latest
const cg = b22.series.use_capital_goods.data, cn = b22.series.use_consumer_non_durables.data;
let capRun = 0; for (let i = cg.length - 1; i >= 0 && cg[i] != null && cn[i] != null && cg[i] > cn[i]; i--) capRun++;
const indRank = Object.values(ind22.series).map((s) => ({ label: s.label.replace(/^Manufacture of /i, "").toLowerCase(), v: s.data.at(-1) })).filter((x) => x.v != null).sort((a, b) => b.v - a.v);
const list = (xs) => xs.slice(0, -1).join(", ") + " and " + xs.at(-1);
// financial years on five rulers: which years output fell, read from the bundles
const FYB = [["1980-81", fy80], ["1993-94", fy93], ["2004-05", fy04], ["2011-12", fy11], ["2022-23", fy22]];
const fyOf = (d) => `${d.slice(0, 4)}-${String((+d.slice(0, 4) + 1) % 100).padStart(2, "0")}`;
const falls = [];
for (const [base, b] of FYB) b.dates.forEach((d, i) => { const v = b.series.general_growth.data[i]; if (v != null && v < 0) falls.push({ fy: fyOf(d), base, v }); });
// the first year with a growth figure (a base's first year has no year before it)
const fy0 = fy80.dates[fy80.series.general_growth.data.findIndex((v) => v != null)];
const fyFirst = fyOf(fy0), fyLast = fyOf(fy22.dates.at(-1));
const fyYears = +fy22.dates.at(-1).slice(0, 4) - +fy0.slice(0, 4) + 1;
const fyNow = fy22.series.general_growth.data.at(-1), fyPrev = fy22.series.general_growth.data.at(-2);
const fyOld = fy11.series.general_growth.data.at(-1);
// the core eight, latest financial year on the current core base
const CI = core11.dates.length - 1, coreFy = fyOf(core11.dates[CI]);
const coreRows = Object.entries(core11.series).filter(([k]) => k.endsWith("_growth") && k !== "composite_growth")
  .map(([k, s]) => ({ k, label: s.label.split(",")[0], v: s.data[CI], w: s.weight })).sort((a, b) => b.v - a.v);
const coreUp = coreRows.filter((r) => r.v > 0), coreDown = coreRows.filter((r) => r.v < 0);
const coreComp = core11.series.composite_growth.data[CI];
const coreYears = +core11.dates[CI].slice(0, 4) - +core93.dates[core93.series.composite_growth.data.findIndex((v) => v != null)].slice(0, 4) + 1;
const fmt1 = (v) => (v < 0 ? "−" : "") + Math.abs(v).toFixed(1) + "%";
---
<Base
  title="Industrial Production · India Atlas"
  description={D.description}
  nav="guides"
  dateStamp={dateStamp(D)}
  crumbLeft={`<a href="/guides/">Guides</a> / ${D.section} / <b>${D.crumbTitle}</b>`}
  crumbRight={`${D.statusNote}, <b>${D.version}</b>`}
  canonical="https://atlas.thisindianlife.today/guides/iip/"
  ogImage={`/og/${D.slug}.png`}
>
<div class="page">

  <div class="titleblock break">
    <div class="kicker">{kicker(D)}</div>
    <div class="hero-grid">
      <h1 set:html={D.headline} />
      <p class="lede">Once a month, the statisticians count what came out of India's mines, factories and power plants, not its value, its volume, and for a few days it is the most argued-about number in the economy. Then it is revised.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="meta">
      <span>Source <b>MoSPI, NSO</b></span>
      <span>Frequency <b>Monthly</b></span>
      <span>Base year <b>2022–23 = 100</b></span>
      <span>Coverage <b>All-India, goods producing sectors</b></span>
      <span>Latest print <b>{P.long}</b></span>
      <span>Released <b>quick estimates</b></span>
    </div>
    <div class="numbers">
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Headline IIP, y/y</div><div class="v acc" id="n-head">–</div><div class="d" id="d-head">–</div><div class="nspark" id="nspark-head"></div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Manufacturing, y/y</div><div class="v" id="n-mfg">–</div><div class="d" id="d-mfg">–</div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Capital goods, y/y</div><div class="v" id="n-cap">–</div><div class="d" id="d-cap">–</div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Electricity &amp; gas, y/y</div><div class="v" id="n-elec">–</div><div class="d" id="d-elec">–</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- § 01 current reading -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">01</span><h2>The current reading</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Quick estimates, {P.long}</span></div>
    <div class="reading-top">
      <div><span class="rk">Current reading</span><div class="big" id="rb-big">–</div></div>
      <p class="rt-cap">Industrial production growth, {P.long}{rankWord ? `: the ${rankWord} month on the current base` : ""}.</p>
      <div class="rt-side"><span class="rk">One month earlier</span><div class="rt-side-v" id="rb-prev">–</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="reading-cols">
      <div class="reading-col">
        <span class="mk">What the print says</span>
        <Prose slot="iip.current-reading.what-the-print-says" />
      </div>
      <div class="reading-col now">
        <span class="mk">What it does not say</span>
        <Prose slot="iip.current-reading.what-it-does-not-say" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 02 register -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">02</span><h2>The register of series</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Latest, year ago, base 2022–23</span></div>
    <div class="break ledger">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Series</th><th>Latest y/y</th><th>vs prev. month</th><th>Year ago</th><th class="spark">2024 to 2026</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody id="ledger-body"></tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 03 two bases -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">03</span><h2>Five ledgers, one index</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">y/y %, five bases, never spliced</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-bases" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Fourteen years of the old ledger, three of the new</h3>
            <div class="sub">IIP general index, year-on-year, base 2011–12 (Apr 2013 to Mar 2026), base 2022–23 (Apr 2024 to {P.short}): plotted side by side, never spliced</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-bases">–</div><div class="c">{P.short}, current base, y/y</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-bases"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, NSO, growth computed from index levels, the two bases share no items panel or weights; the gap between them is a change of ruler, not a change of output</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>The long grey line is the retired base: a decade of single-digit hum, then the cliff of April 2020 (−57%) and the mirror-image spike of April 2021 (+134%): the two wildest prints in Indian statistics, both arithmetic as much as event. The blue line is the current base, begun April 2023. Where they overlap, they disagree by a point or two. That disagreement is not an error; it is what a new basket and a new panel measure.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {P.stamp}</span>
            <p>The current base has now produced {word(gen.length)} months of growth readings{nNeg === 0 ? ", every one of them positive" : nNeg === 1 ? ", all but one of them positive" : `, all but ${word(nNeg)} of them positive`}. The old base was published for the last time with the March 2026 print. Its March 2025 index, missing from the source for months, has since been loaded, so the old series closes without a gap.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break" style="margin-top:104px">
      <figure id="cb-herit" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>The deep ledger: three retired rulers, 1982 to 2017</h3>
            <div class="sub">IIP general index, year-on-year, base 1980–81 (Apr 1982 to Mar 1998), base 1993–94 (Apr 1995 to Jun 2011), base 2004–05 (Apr 2006 to Mar 2017): side by side, never spliced</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-herit">–</div><div class="c">Feb 2009, the one industrial winter</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-herit"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, NSO (bases 1993–94 and 2004–05); CSO's base 1980–81 as republished by the RBI (DBIE), which MoSPI's API does not serve. Growth computed from index levels. Where two rulers overlap they read the same months differently, a change of ruler, not of output</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Beneath the modern pair lie the three rulers India retired earlier: 1980–81, 1993–94 and 2004–05. Together they carry the monthly record back more than four decades: the steady eighties, the stall of 1991 when a balance-of-payments crisis choked imports of parts and fuel, the late-nineties grind, the 2003–2008 boom and the thin winter that followed. Where the lines overlap, from 1995 to 1998 and again from 2006 to 2011, they measure the same factories and disagree, sometimes by whole points. Neither is wrong; each holds a different basket.</p>
            <p>Read these lines by the year, not the month. The item panels behind the old bases were smaller, so a single lumpy order could move an aggregate: capital goods once printed a 65 per cent year-on-year jump on one month's reading. The monthly sawtooth is arithmetic on a small panel; the slow drift underneath it is the economy.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of the final prints</span>
            <p>The sharpest lesson sits in February 2009. The 2004–05 base printed <b id="her-gfc04">–</b>: the deepest reading any of these rulers gave. The 1993–94 base read the same month at <b id="her-gfc93">–</b>, and in seventeen years it never once printed below zero. Same factories, one winter, two verdicts. The 1980–81 base, the oldest, touched its low of <b id="her-low80">–</b> in <span id="her-low80-at">–</span> and signed off in March 1998 at <b id="her-end80">–</b>; the 1993–94 base in June 2011 at <b id="her-end93">–</b>; the 2004–05 base in March 2017 at <b id="her-end04">–</b>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 04 financial years, every ruler -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">04</span><h2>{fyYears} years in financial years</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">annual average index, growth on a year earlier, five bases</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-fy" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>{falls.length === 0 ? "Not one financial year of falling output" : `Output fell in ${word(new Set(falls.map((f) => f.fy)).size)} financial years out of ${fyYears}`}</h3>
            <div class="sub">IIP general index, growth of the financial-year average on the year before, per cent, {fyFirst} to {fyLast}, five bases drawn side by side and never spliced</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-fy">–</div><div class="c">{fyLast}, base 2022–23</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-fy"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, NSO, annual rows of the IIP (bases 1993–94 to 2022–23); CSO's base 1980–81 as republished by the RBI (DBIE report 219). Each year is the average of its twelve months; growth is the published rate (computed from the averages for 1980–81). The 1993–94 base publishes no growth for 2004–05</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>A month is noisy; a year is not. The financial-year average smooths away festivals, strikes and one-off orders, and leaves the thing a household feels over time: whether there is more steel, cement, cloth and power in the country this year than last. Each dot is one year on one ruler. Where two rulers cover the same year they usually agree on direction and differ on size, because each weighs a different basket of goods.</p>
            <p>A year of growth near 3 per cent and a year near 9 per cent are very different worlds for a factory worker: one is a plant running a single shift, the other a plant hiring for a second.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {fyLast}</span>
            <p>{fyLast} grew {fmt1(fyNow)} on the current base, after {fmt1(fyPrev)} the year before; the retired 2011–12 base, in its last year, read {fyLast} at {fmt1(fyOld)}. {falls.length ? `Output fell in ${list(falls.map((f) => `${f.fy} (${fmt1(f.v)}, base ${f.base})`))}.` : ""} The 2013–14 fall shows only on the 2004–05 base: the 2011–12 base reads the same year as growth. Two rulers, one year, opposite signs.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 05 the long ledger: 23 industries on the retired base -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">05</span><h2>The long ledger: 23 industries</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">y/y %, base 2011–12, monthly</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-old" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>What the retired base remembers</h3>
            <div class="sub">All 23 two-digit manufacturing industries, year-on-year, per cent, Apr 2013 to Mar 2026, ordered by the final print</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-old">–</div><div class="c">industries beyond ±10%, Mar 2026</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" style="padding-top:26px">
            <div id="grid-old" style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(240px,1fr));gap:28px 36px"></div>
          </div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, NSO, y-axes clipped at −40 / +45: the 2020–21 lockdown prints run to −99% and past +1000%, off scale by design, blue marks a final print beyond ±10%, the disclosed threshold, Mar 2025 was never loaded, so Feb 2026 is the last computable month, full values in the CSV</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Each panel is one manufacturing industry on the retired 2011–12 base: the longest industrial memory the monthly statistics keep. The panels share one clipped scale, so a flat grey hum means a steady industry and a wide sawtooth means a lumpy one: a single large plant's shutdown can swing a two-digit industry by double digits. The lockdown spike is clipped out of every panel on purpose; it is the same arithmetic everywhere and teaches nothing about any one industry.</p>
            <p>This grid is memory, not news. The current 2022–23 base lives in the plates above, and the two are never spliced: an industry's panel here ends where the old ledger ended.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of the final prints</span>
            <p>The old base signed off in February 2026 with the split it had shown for two years: <b id="old-hi-name">–</b> fastest at <b id="old-hi">–</b>, <b id="old-lo-name">–</b> slowest at <b id="old-lo">–</b>. In its last month, <b id="old-n">–</b> of the 23 industries were moving faster than ±10%: the average never described them.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 06 plate 1 -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">06</span><h2>Plate 1: the sectors</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">y/y %, base 2022–23, monthly</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-main" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Mining, manufacturing, power: the three engines, plus the mains</h3>
            <div class="sub">IIP by sector, year-on-year, per cent, Apr 2024 to {P.short}</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-main">–</div><div class="c">{P.short}, headline y/y</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="legend" id="legend-main">
            <button class="chip" data-key="general"><i style="background:#2E4BD8"></i>General</button>
            <button class="chip" data-key="sector_manufacturing"><i style="background:#5F6675"></i>Manufacturing</button>
            <button class="chip" data-key="sector_mining_quarrying"><i style="background:#A3A9B4"></i>Mining</button>
            <button class="chip" data-key="sector_electricity_gas_supply"><i style="background:#878D99"></i>Electricity &amp; gas</button>
            <button class="chip" data-key="sector_water_supply_sewerage_waste_management"><i style="background:#C9CED6"></i>Water &amp; waste</button>
          </div>
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-main"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, NSO, the latest months are quick estimates, revised in later releases, water supply, sewerage &amp; waste management is the fourth sector, new on this base, it runs near the headline but is shown here for completeness</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Manufacturing is the heavyweight, most of the index, so the blue general line shadows the dark one. Mining and electricity swing wider and mean less individually. When the three point the same way, as they have through 2026, the print is broad-based; when they scatter, the headline is an average of disagreement.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {P.stamp}</span>
            <p>Manufacturing ({pct(last("sector_manufacturing"))}) and electricity ({pct(last("sector_electricity_gas_supply"))}) carry {P.month}'s print. Mining is the drag at {pct(last("sector_mining_quarrying"))} year on year: a sector that answers to the monsoon and to administrative clearance more than to demand.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 07 the core eight -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">07</span><h2>The core eight</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">annual, base 2011–12 = 100, and two retired bases</span></div>
    <div class="wm-grid" style="margin-bottom:56px">
      <div>
        <p>Eight industries sit under almost everything else India makes: coal, crude oil, natural gas, refinery products, fertilisers, steel, cement and electricity. A new flat needs cement and steel. A farm needs fertiliser. A factory, a hospital and a phone charger need electricity, and most of that electricity still comes from coal. The Office of the Economic Adviser publishes an index of these eight every month, about a week before the IIP for the same month, which is why markets read it as an early hint of where the IIP is heading.</p>
        <p>Together they carry about two-fifths of the IIP's weight on the 2011–12 base (40.27 of 100), so a bad month for coal or power drags the whole index. What follows is the RBI's annual record of that index: financial-year averages and their growth, on three bases that are never joined.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="wm-rail">
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note</span>
          <b>Monthly figures are not here yet.</b> The RBI's database, our source for this index, keeps only the annual table. The monthly series lives with the Office of the Economic Adviser; we do not yet carry it.
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-core" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>{coreRows[0].label} and {coreRows[1].label.toLowerCase()} carried the core in {coreFy}; {list(coreDown.slice(-2).map((r) => r.label.toLowerCase()))} shrank</h3>
            <div class="sub">The eight core industries, growth of the financial-year average on the year before, per cent, {coreFy}, base 2011–12, with each industry's weight in the IIP</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-core">–</div><div class="c">{coreFy}, composite index</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-core"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: Office of the Economic Adviser, DPIIT, Index of Eight Core Industries, as published by the RBI (DBIE reports 499 and 989). Growth as published. Weights: each industry's weight in the IIP, base 2011–12</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://eaindustry.nic.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Read the bars with their weights. Refinery products weigh 11.29 in the IIP, cement 2.16: a one-point slip in refining moves the index five times as much as the same slip in cement. Steel and cement rise together when India builds roads, railways and houses; crude oil and natural gas have fallen for most of the last decade because the old fields are ageing, which is why the country imports most of its oil.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {coreFy}</span>
            <p>The composite grew {fmt1(coreComp)} in {coreFy}. {cap(word(coreUp.length))} of the eight grew and {word(coreDown.length)} shrank: {list(coreUp.map((r) => `${r.label.toLowerCase()} ${fmt1(r.v)}`))} against {list(coreDown.map((r) => `${r.label.toLowerCase()} ${fmt1(r.v)}`))}. The building materials did the work; the fuels did not.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break" style="margin-top:104px">
      <figure id="cb-corelong" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>{coreYears} years of the core, on three rulers: one year of contraction</h3>
            <div class="sub">Composite index of the eight core industries, growth of the financial-year average, per cent, bases 1993–94 (1995–96 to 2010–11), 2004–05 (2005–06 to 2013–14) and 2011–12 (2013–14 to {coreFy}), side by side, never spliced; the IIP general index (base 2011–12) for comparison</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-corelong">–</div><div class="c">2020–21, base 2011–12</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-corelong"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: OEA, DPIIT, as published by the RBI (DBIE 499, 989); IIP: MoSPI, NSO. The 1993–94 base's first-year growth (1994–95) is measured by the RBI against the 1980–81 base's level and is left out; the 2004–05 base's growth stops in 2013–14 in the RBI's table</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://data.rbi.org.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>The core index and the IIP move together because the core is two-fifths of the IIP, but the core swings less: power and fuel are bought every day, in good years and bad, while cars and machines can wait. When the IIP falls harder than the core, households and firms are postponing purchases, not switching off the lights.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {coreFy}</span>
            <p>On every ruler, the core grew in every financial year but one: 2020–21, the lockdown year, when the composite fell <b id="core-2020">–</b> and the IIP <b id="iip-2020">–</b>. Where the 1993–94 and 2004–05 rulers overlap, from 2005–06 to 2010–11, they disagree by as much as <b id="core-gap">–</b> in a single year.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 08 the measure -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">08</span><h2>What this measures</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="wm-grid">
      <div>
        <p>The IIP counts physical output: tonnes of coal, metres of cloth, kilowatt-hours, scooters rolling off lines. Not revenue, not profit, not value added: volume. When we say industry grew 7.3 per cent, we mean the index of physical production stands <b>7.3 per cent above the same month a year ago</b>.</p>
        <p>It is built from a fixed panel of representative items, each standing in for a slice of its industry, weighted together on a 2022–23 frame. It arrives fast, quick estimates within weeks, and the price of speed is revision: late data from factories trickles in for two months, and the first print is a draft.</p>
        <p>It is the oldest high-frequency read on the real economy India has, which is why everyone over-reads it. The honest use is narrow: direction and breadth, in three-month moving windows, never point estimates.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="wm-rail">
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, i</span>
          <b>Volume, not value.</b> A price spike can double an industry's revenue while its IIP contribution does nothing. This index deliberately ignores prices.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, ii</span>
          <b>Quick estimates are drafts.</b> The first print is revised as late factory returns arrive. Compare vintages, not headlines.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, iii</span>
          <b>Growth is computed from levels.</b> The published growth rate is rounded; ours is recomputed from the index and tracks it within 0.05 pt.
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 09 component plates -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">09</span><h2>The parts, one plate each</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">y/y %, base 2022–23</span></div>

    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-use" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Machines or soap: the use-based read</h3>
            <div class="sub">Capital goods vs consumer goods, year-on-year, per cent, Apr 2024 to {P.short}</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-use">–</div><div class="c">{P.short}, capital goods y/y</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-use"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, NSO, use-based classification regroups the same factories by what their output is for</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Same index, different sorting: by what the output is <em>for</em>. Capital goods track the investment cycle: factories buying machines. Consumer durables track the confident household; non-durables, the everyday one. When capital runs ahead of consumer for a year, the expansion is investment-led, and the question becomes when the consumer joins.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {P.stamp}</span>
            <p>Capital goods at <b id="ex-cap">–</b> against consumer non-durables at <b id="ex-cnd">–</b>: capital goods have grown faster for {word(capRun)} months running. The consumption line swings from month to month, so read the gap over several prints, not any one of them.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break" style="margin-top:104px">
      <figure id="cb-ind" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Inside manufacturing: the fastest and the slowest</h3>
            <div class="sub">Selected 2-digit industries, year-on-year, per cent, Apr 2024 to {P.short}</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-ind">–</div><div class="c">{PI.short}, electrical equipment y/y</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-ind"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, NSO, four of the 23 manufacturing industries on the current base, chosen by June rank</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>The aggregate hides a split economy. Electrical equipment, transformers, switchgear, the grid build-out, and motor vehicles are running hot; apparel and wood products are shrinking. Averages describe none of these industries. This is why the register of industries matters more than the headline.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {PI.stamp}</span>
            <p>Electrical equipment grew <b id="ex-elec">–</b> in {PI.month} and motor vehicles <b id="ex-mv">–</b>, while wearing apparel contracted <b id="ex-app">–</b>. The boom is in the capital-goods spine of manufacturing; the weakness sits in export-facing consumer lines.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break" style="margin-top:104px">
      <figure id="cb-ind22" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Every industry on the current base: the live field, ranked</h3>
            <div class="sub">All <b id="ind-n">–</b> two-digit industries, year-on-year, per cent, base 2022–23, Apr 2024 to {PI.short}, ordered by the latest print, blue beyond ±10%</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="ind-hot">–</div><div class="c">industries beyond ±10%, {PI.short}</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div id="grid-ind22" style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(240px,1fr));gap:28px 36px;padding:20px 0 8px"></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, IIP on base 2022–23, every two-digit industry the current base serves, shared clipped scale −20 to +45%; the CSV carries full values, the freshest detail the index offers</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="csv">CSV, all series</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>The retired base's 23-industry ledger (§05) is a memory; this is the live field. Each panel is one industry on its own clock against a shared scale, ordered by the latest print. Read the rows, not the lines: where the current expansion sits, and where it does not reach.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {PI.stamp}</span>
            <p>The hottest industry is <b id="ind-hi">–</b>; the coolest <b id="ind-lo">–</b>. The top three are {list(indRank.slice(0, 3).map((x) => x.label))}; the bottom three {list(indRank.slice(-3).map((x) => x.label))}. A panel past the blue threshold is moving at double digits.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 10 the services counterpart -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">10</span><h2>The services counterpart: ISP</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Index levels, base 2024–25 = 100, trial series</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-isp" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>A monthly index for services, at last</h3>
            <div class="sub">Index of Services Production, five of nineteen series, index levels, base 2024–25 = 100, {PISP.span}</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-isp">–</div><div class="c">{PISP.short}, retail trade index</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="legend" id="legend-isp">
            <button class="chip" data-key="retail_trade"><i style="background:#2E4BD8"></i>Retail trade</button>
            <button class="chip" data-key="road_transport"><i style="background:#878D99"></i>Road transport</button>
            <button class="chip" data-key="banking"><i style="background:#5F6675"></i>Banking</button>
            <button class="chip" data-key="telecommunication"><i style="background:#C9CED6"></i>Telecommunication</button>
            <button class="chip" data-key="it_computer_related_services"><i style="background:#A3A9B4"></i>IT &amp; computer services</button>
          </div>
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-isp"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, NSO, trial series, first published 2025, methodology may change without notice, dashed rule: 100, the base-year average, levels, not growth rates: no year-on-year exists before Apr 2026</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>The IIP counts goods. Services, more than half of everything India produces, had no monthly output measure at all: between one GDP quarter and the next, the largest part of the economy was statistically dark. The Index of Services Production is the answer, built like the IIP but counting bank transactions, freight, retail sales and the rest.</p>
            <p>Read positions, not slopes. Each line is a level against its 2024–25 average: above 100 is above the base year, and that is all. The sawtooth is unmodelled seasonality: this index is too young for anyone to know what a normal October or a normal March looks like, so a month-to-month jump is not information yet. We do not know its seasonal shape. Nobody does; the history required to learn it does not exist.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {PISP.stamp}</span>
            <p>Retail trade stood at <b id="isp-rt">–</b> in {PISP.long}: <b id="isp-rt-yy">–</b> above {PISP.month} {+PISP.year - 1}, comparing like month with like, the only comparison the young series supports. Banking read <b id="isp-bk">–</b>. Beyond that the honest commentary runs out fast: insurance printed <b id="isp-ins-mar">–</b> in March and <b id="isp-ins-may">–</b> in {PISP.month}. Year-end accounting, seasonality, or noise: we do not know, because no earlier March exists to compare.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>

      <div class="ledger" style="margin-top:64px">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Series</th><th id="isp-th-latest">Latest level</th><th id="isp-th-yoy">vs year ago</th><th id="isp-th-year">Year ago</th><th class="spark">{PISP.span}</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody id="isp-ledger-body"></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-top:64px">
        <div class="plate-head" style="margin-bottom:16px">
          <div class="t">
            <h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-weight:500;font-size:1.3rem">All nineteen services, by cluster</h3>
            <div class="sub">Every ISP series as a small multiple, index levels, base 2024–25 = 100, grouped: trade, logistics, finance, ICT, contact-intensive, professional, {PISP.span}</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div id="grid-isp" style="padding:8px 0"></div>
        <p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.6px;color:#878D99;margin-top:16px;line-height:1.8">Each panel: the service's index level over fourteen months against 100, the base-year average. The percentage is the like-month change from May 2025. Sawtooth is unmodelled seasonality: the series is too young for anyone to know its calendar shape.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 11 misread -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">11</span><h2>How to misread this number</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <ul class="misread">
      <li>
        <span class="no">i.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"Industry grew 7%, so the economy grew 7%."</h3>
          <p>Industry is under a third of GDP, and this index covers only its organised, goods-producing slice. Services, the larger half of the economy, are elsewhere entirely. The IIP is a pulse, not the patient.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">ii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"One strong month is a boom."</h3>
          <p>This is the most volatile major series in Indian statistics. A festival calendar shift can move a print by two points; a single large factory's shutdown can move an industry. Three-month averages are the minimum unit of belief.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">iii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"The first print is the number."</h3>
          <p>Quick estimates are revised as late returns arrive, and the revisions are not noise around zero: they cluster. Citing the first print of two years ago against the final print of last year mixes vintages.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">iv.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"Growth recovered, so output recovered."</h3>
          <p>Every growth rate is a ratio to a year ago. In April 2021 the index printed +134%: and output was still below its pre-pandemic level. The spike measured the hole in the denominator, not the height of the economy.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">v.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"The two bases tell different stories, so one is wrong."</h3>
          <p>The 2011–12 and 2022–23 series use different items, different factories, different weights. Where they overlap they routinely disagree by a point. That is what changing the ruler does. Compare within a base, never across the seam.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">vi.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"The core index fell, so the IIP will fall."</h3>
          <p>The eight core industries are about two-fifths of the IIP. The other three-fifths, from medicines to motorcycles, can move the other way, and often do. The core is an early hint, not a forecast.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">vii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"A young index can be read like an old one."</h3>
          <p>A new index, the ISP is one, has no seasonal history: nobody knows what its normal festival month or year-end looks like, because none has happened twice. Until the same month has recurred for years, it supports statements about levels, not about momentum. Where the seasonal shape is unknown, the honest reading is "we do not know".</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

